  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates a refusal-semantics steering baseline on VLMs and reports harmful-output outcomes. This is a conceptual test of steering-related refusal behavior, not a reproduction of the original difference-in-means construction or its all-layer, all-position ablation.",
    "gate_note": "The Refusal Pairs baseline is the relevant reported intervention: the authors say it shifts outputs toward refusal and report toxicity and attack-success rates. ASTRA's own image-attribution direction and its utility results are not results for the claim's refusal direction, so they are not included as claim-version rows. The paper also reports Jailbreak Templates comparator results, but those do not test a refusal direction. No results test refusal induction on harmless prompts.",
    "test_reason": "The paper builds ASTRA, a VLM jailbreak defense that steers away from an image-attribution 'harmful' direction, and reports mainly its own performance. Its only refusal-related intervention is the 'Refusal Pairs' baseline. That baseline follows Rimsky et al.'s contrastive activation addition (CAA) at one layer, not the harmful-minus-harmless difference-in-means direction. It is scored by toxicity and ASR under adversarial images, not by refusal rates, and is reported only as a comparator. No ablation, refusal induction on harmless prompts, or capability check is reported for it. The claim that refusal steering 'indiscriminately increases refusal rates' is cited from Arditi et al., not measured here. So the paper uses refusal steering as a tool or comparator without testing any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that ASTRA's adaptive image-attribution steering defends VLMs against jailbreaks better and more cheaply than VLM defenses and LLM steering baselines, while keeping benign utility. The statement that refusal steering 'indiscriminately increases refusal rates' and harms utility is asserted with a citation to Arditi et al. It is not measured for the Refusal Pairs baseline; the only utility measurement of fixed linear steering uses ASTRA's own vector (Fig. 8).",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The evaluated Refusal Pairs comparator is a textual refusal-semantics steering baseline, not the original harmful-minus-harmless difference-in-means direction or the original all-layer, all-position ablation. The paper's main ASTRA method instead derives directions from attributed visual tokens and applies calibrated adaptive steering.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary across model and perturbation strength. The paper says fixed linear steering degrades benign utility; its own adaptive method depends on calibration, steering coefficient, and layer selection.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "CAA-style refusal-pair steering at one layer partly lowers toxicity and ASR in three VLMs under PGD image attacks. ASR falls about 20–37 points; Qwen2-VL toxicity falls only about 4–13 points.",
        "proposed_claim": "Text-derived refusal steering vectors (CAA) carry over to VLMs under adversarial-image jailbreaks, but only partially reduce harmful output.",
